Hi Fabio

Copying to the tsdyn mailing list, as might interest other people.

Well there are tow issues here. The slow function is due to the fact that a two dimensional grid-search is run each time. So it increases (linearly) with the number of bootstrap, and increases (quadratically) with the sample size! Note one could run instead a conditional grid search as in Lo and Zivot, which would reduce the algorithm speed from n^2 to 2n, so quite a huge improvement. Unfortunately, I haven't found the time to do so...

Now for the computational failures. This comes from the fact that one is using lags, which can be highly multicollinear, and the messages you see are the  usual messages in case of failures to invert (close to) singular matrices. This seems very difficult to avoid, but on the other side, having a "error-tolerant"  (i.e. just discard errors, do not stop) approach should not be too difficult, and I can try to do this in the next (few) week(s)!

Then I'll be very interested in hearing about your Monte-Carlo simulations! What would be most useful in case you are running MC would be to run also the MC used in SEO, which would allow to compare directly results with his code and mine?

Best

Matthieu

